  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Edson grew up around Lake Washington, built a small craft with paper-route earnings and raced as a teenager. After military service, he began selling used racing equipment from a Seattle parking lot. He expanded Advanced Outboard Marine into retail stores, purchased the Bayliner name and developed standardised production aimed at affordable family recreation.\n\nHis decisive innovation was a complete boat, motor and trailer package sold at a clear value price. Manufacturing scale, limited options and worker incentives supported rapid growth while maintaining quality. Brunswick acquired Bayliner in 1986, after which Edson invested across financial assets and real estate and supported entrepreneurship education. His commercial legacy rests on turning a specialist purchase into a widely accessible consumer product.",
    "html": "<p>Edson grew up around Lake Washington, built a small craft with paper-route earnings and raced as a teenager. After military service, he began selling used racing equipment from a Seattle parking lot. He expanded Advanced Outboard Marine into retail stores, purchased the Bayliner name and developed standardised production aimed at affordable family recreation.</p>\r\n<p>His decisive innovation was a complete boat, motor and trailer package sold at a clear value price. Manufacturing scale, limited options and worker incentives supported rapid growth while maintaining quality. Brunswick acquired Bayliner in 1986, after which Edson invested across financial assets and real estate and supported entrepreneurship education. His commercial legacy rests on turning a specialist purchase into a widely accessible consumer product.</p>\r\n",
